  "worked_solution": "Newton\u2019s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t),\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{t})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot \u27e8-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t),\\,-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =\u27e8-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot 2),\\,-\\frac{2}{(2)^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(2)| & =\\sqrt{(-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4))^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & \u22486\\,\\text{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the magnitude of the resultant force is $6\\,\\text{N}.$",
